<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Grenada</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1974 CE · capital St George's</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British colony on Grenada, the Isle of Spice, shaken by Fédon's rebellion and capital of the Windward Islands, independent since 1974.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-grenada">French Grenada</a> in 1762 and received it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1763. Many French planters stayed, and British attempts to restrict Catholic rights caused lasting tension. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> retook the island in 1779 but returned it in 1783. Under British rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">Fort Royal</a> became St George's.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> estates worked by enslaved Africans dominated until emancipation. In 1795 to 1796 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julien-fedon">Julien Fédon</a>, a free coloured planter inspired by revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, led a rebellion that held most of the island and killed the lieutenant governor. In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> replaced sugar, giving <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> its spice reputation, and from 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georges-grenada">St George's</a> housed the governor of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-windward-islands">British Windward Islands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown colony</a> rule replaced the assembly in 1877.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-gairy">Eric Gairy's</a> labour movement of 1951 brought mass politics. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a>, became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies-associated-states">Associated State</a> in 1967 and, despite strikes and protests against Gairy, gained independence as Grenada on 7 February 1974.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1763 CE – 1974 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.79%;width:4.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1974 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>St George's</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Julien Fédon, Eric Gairy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Grenadian Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/grenada">Grenada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Grenada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=colony-of-grenada" aria-label="Colony of Grenada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2983 1728 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Grenada lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3303 1984L3304 1983M3303 1984L4997 651M3303 1984L3365 1946M3303 1984L3305 2036M3303 1984L3304 1983"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3304 1983h0M4997 651h0M3365 1946h0M3305 2036h0M3304 1983h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3303 1984h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3303 1984h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks St George'